INT. SMALL-TOWN PET FUNERAL PARLOR - DAY

Pastel walls. Little urns on velvet shelves. A framed poster: "Every Goodbye Deserves Dignity."

BEATRIX "BEA" LANGSTROM-OYELEYE (40s, funeral director, black pencil skirt, too-bright lipstick) stands at a small oak lectern with a sleeping ferret in a tiny casket beside her.

Seated in the front row, in folding chairs: GERALDINE "GERRY" POTTS (60s, widowed, tissue in fist) and her brother-in-law HECTOR MWENDA-ABERNATHY (60s, loud tie, sunglasses on indoors).

BEA
Friends. We are gathered to honor Nibbles.

GERRY
(weeping)
Oh, Nibbles.

HECTOR
He was a good ferret.

BEA
He was.

HECTOR
Smelled like a gym bag.

GERRY
Hector!

HECTOR
I'm saying it with love.

BEA
Perhaps we could hold the anecdotes until the reception.

HECTOR
I'm just saying he bit the mailman.

GERRY
He bit the mailman once.

HECTOR
Four times.

GERRY
Twice.

HECTOR
Four. I counted.

BEA
(over them)
Let us bow our heads.

They do. A pause. A phone rings, tinny, from Hector's pocket.

HECTOR
(answering)
Not now, Dennis.

DENNIS (O.S.)
(through the phone, blaring)
Hector, did you tell her about the hamster?

GERRY
What hamster?

HECTOR
Dennis, I'm at a funeral!

DENNIS (O.S.)
Whose?

BEA
Nibbles's.

DENNIS (O.S.)
Oh no. He was a good ferret!

HECTOR
That's what I said!

GERRY
Hector, what hamster?

HECTOR
Nothing! A rumor!

BEA
Ladies, gentlemen, caller, please--

The casket lid pops open. Nibbles sits up, yawns, stretches.

Silence in every key.

GERRY
He's alive.

HECTOR
He was asleep.

BEA
(holding the lectern)
It happens.

DENNIS (O.S.)
What happens? Did he come back?

Hector hangs up. Gerry scoops the ferret into her arms and sobs with joy.

GERRY
My boy! My Nibbles!

BEA
(recovering)
I'll prepare a refund.

HECTOR
Keep the flowers.

Nibbles bites Bea. She yelps. Hector nods, satisfied.

HECTOR (CONT'D)
Four times.
